UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 32, Lesson 1: Voice, gesture and expression', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Performance skills combine clear voice (articulation, volume, pace), intentional gesture and facial expression that match the objective. Too many gestures blur meaning; choose fewer, clearer actions. Warm up voice gently; stop if throat hurts.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nA line is important but mumbled with busy hands. Give two fixes.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Articulate consonants and slow pace; reduce to one clear gesture.''\n\nWATCH FOR\nShouting equals good projection. Clarity and support matter more than strain.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nPerform eight lines focusing on voice + one gesture each. Record (optional) or peer-check clarity.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"Performance skills combine clear voice (articulation, volume, pace), intentional gesture and facial expression that match the objective. Too many gestures blur meaning; choose fewer, clearer actions. Warm up voice gently; stop if throat hurts.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Explore and learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-DRA-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Perform eight lines focusing on voice + one gesture each. Record (optional) or peer-check clarity. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''Articulate consonants and slow pace; reduce to one clear gesture.'' Reasoning: I used voice by attending to gesture rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Shouting equals good projection. Clarity and support matter more than strain. Correction: Strong answer: ''Articulate consonants and slow pace; reduce to one clear gesture.'' Why: the correction restores accurate use of voice.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Shouting equals good projection. Clarity and support matter more than strain. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating voice on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with voice, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''Articulate consonants and slow pace; reduce to one clear gesture.'' This matches the lesson skill for voice.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Strong answer: ''Articulate consonants and slow pace; reduce to one clear gesture.''","Shouting equals good projection. Clarity and support matter more than strain."],"question":"A line is important but mumbled with busy hands. Give two fixes. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Shouting equals good projection. Clarity and support matter more than strain. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Careful use of gesture with clear evidence.","Shouting equals good projection. Clarity and support matter more than strain.","Performance skills combine clear voice (articulation, volume, pace), intentional gesture and facial expression that match the objective. Too many gestures…","Strong answer: ''Articulate consonants and slow pace; reduce to one clear gesture.''"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising voice?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named gesture, not by copying the model.","options":["Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the gesture used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right."],"question":"A learner is practising voice. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answer: ''Articulate consonants and slow pace; reduce to one clear gesture.'' uses elements, techniques and audience effect appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Strong answer: ''Articulate consonants and slow pace; reduce to one clear gesture.''","Shouting equals good projection. Clarity and support matter more than strain.","Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about voice?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Shouting equals good projection. Clarity and support matter more than strain.","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about voice."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about gesture?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Say it looks nice with no element or technique named.","Example: To plead, an actor slows pace, softens volume, opens hands once—not flailing. Expression matches the ask.","Shouting equals good projection. Clarity and support matter more than strain.","Invent a sacred First Nations design without permission or guidance."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Performance skills combine clear voice (articulation, volume, pace), intentional gesture and facial… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example artwork or script and call that independent work.","Shouting equals good projection. Clarity and support matter more than strain.","Perform eight lines focusing on voice + one gesture each. Record (optional) or peer-check clarity."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of voice?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating voice on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Strong answer: ''Articulate consonants and slow pace; reduce to one clear gesture.'' Reasoning: I used voice by attending to gesture rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with voice, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Shouting equals good projection. Clarity and support matter more than strain. Correction: Strong answer: ''Articulate consonants and slow pace; reduce to one clear gesture.'' Why: the correction restores accurate use of voice.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y4-w32-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise voice.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Drama","success_criteria":["I can use voice with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify gesture and explain why it matters.","I can practise expression independently on a new prompt.","I can notice clarity needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Performance skills combine clear voice (articulation, volume, pace), intentional gesture and facial expression that match the objective. Too many gestures blur meaning; choose fewer, clearer actions. Warm up voice gently; stop if throat hurts. Supported responses name the…","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","Performance skills combine clear voice (articulation, volume, pace), intentional gesture and facial expression that match the objective. Too many gestures blur meaning; choose fewer, clearer actions. Warm up voice gently; stop if throat hurts.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about voice?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: To plead, an actor slows pace, softens volume, opens hands once—not flailing. Expression matches the ask.\n\nTRY FIRST\nA line is important but mumbled with busy hands. Give two fixes.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Articulate consonants and slow pace; reduce to one clear gesture.''","explain":"Performance skills combine clear voice (articulation, volume, pace), intentional gesture and facial expression that match the objective. Too many gestures blur meaning; choose fewer, clearer actions. Warm up voice gently; stop if throat hurts.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Shouting equals good projection. Clarity and support matter more than strain. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Performance skills.","caption":"Choose on purpose.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Voice</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">clear pace volume</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Gesture</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">few and clear</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: To plead, an actor slows pace, softens volume, opens hands once—not flailing. Expression matches the ask. Then write your own artistic reason.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which artistic choice matters most.\nJuno: Name the element, technique or performance choice and the effect it creates before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: To plead, an actor slows pace, softens volume, opens hands once—not flailing. Expression matches the ask. Then write your own artistic reason."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about voice?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: To plead, an actor slows pace, softens volume, opens hands once—not flailing. Expression matches the ask. Look at each line, sound, movement or role choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key artistic choices from the example and say what each one does for the audience or artwork.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful response names the element or technique and its effect, not only “it looks nice”.","explain":"Make the arts idea visible: name the element, technique or performance choice you used and say what effect it creates.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final praise word without naming a choice does not show arts thinking. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Show the artistic choice","visual":[{"alt":"Voice care.","caption":"No strain.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Warm-up</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">gentle</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Stop</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">if throat hurts</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about voice?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nShouting equals good projection. Clarity and support matter more than strain. Here is a more helpful example: Example: To plead, an actor slows pace, softens volume, opens hands once—not flailing. Expression matches the ask.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Articulate consonants and slow pace; reduce to one clear gesture.''","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Shouting equals good projection. Clarity and support matter more than strain. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice. Supported responses name the element or technique and its effect.","options":["A guess with no artistic reason.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about voice?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPerformance skills combine clear voice (articulation, volume, pace), intentional gesture and facial expression that match the objective. Too many gestures blur meaning; choose fewer, clearer actions. Warm up voice gently; stop if throat hurts. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with different content or material.","explain":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example. Review clarity, safety and respectful practice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new artwork or performance. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Transfer to a new artwork or performance"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 32, Lesson 1: Voice, gesture and expression","unit_number":7,"unit_title":"Drama: scripts and theatre","week":32,"week_in_unit":2,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: To plead, an actor slows pace, softens volume, opens hands once—not flailing. Expression matches the ask.\n\nWrite what makes this example effective, naming the element, technique or performance choice and its effect.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Performance skills.","caption":"Choose on purpose.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Voice</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">clear pace volume</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Gesture</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">few and clear</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Voice care.","caption":"No strain.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\